# USSLP label firmware configuration.
#
# The options here are the ones a deployment actually has to choose between, not
# a knob for every constant. Anything that is a property of the hardware lives
# in the devicetree; anything that is a property of the platform's contracts is
# not configurable at all.

menu "USSLP smart shelf label"

config USSLP_DISPLAY_TIER
	int "Display tier fitted to this board"
	default 0
	range 0 2
	help
	  0 = 2.9in 296x128 BWR, 1 = 4.2in 400x300 BW, 2 = 5.85in 600x448 ACeP.
	  The numbering is labelsim.DisplayTier's, so a tier in a planogram, in the
	  simulator and in this firmware are the same integer.

	  This is baked into the image and checked against the OTA manifest: an
	  image built for one panel must never be swapped onto another, because the
	  waveform tables are different and the wrong waveform can leave a panel
	  permanently marked.

config USSLP_EINK_PLANES
	int "Ink planes the panel controller is loaded with"
	default 2
	range 0 2
	help
	  Two for the BWR panel (a black plane and a red plane, one bit each), one
	  for the plain black and white panel, zero for the seven-colour panel,
	  which is streamed rather than buffered.

config USSLP_EINK_PLANE_BYTES
	int "Bytes in one packed ink plane"
	default 4736
	help
	  width*height/8, rounded up. Set per tier in boards/tier_*.conf. The
	  framebuffer is the largest RAM allocation in the firmware, which is why it
	  is packed here and one byte per pixel on the controller.

config USSLP_EINK_STREAMING
	bool "Stream the image to the panel controller instead of buffering it"
	default n
	help
	  The 5.85-inch seven-colour panel is 134 KB at four bits per pixel, which
	  does not fit alongside the radio stacks in 256 KB. In streaming mode the
	  decoder writes bands straight into the panel controller's own RAM as they
	  arrive from the mesh. The cost is that there is no retained local copy to
	  diff against, which is one of the two reasons that panel cannot do a
	  partial refresh.

menuconfig USSLP_ZIGBEE
	bool "Zigbee 3.0 primary radio"
	default y
	help
	  The primary uplink: an IEEE 802.15.4 mesh through the TI CC2652P
	  co-processor to the Shelf Edge Controller. Requires the ZBOSS stack from
	  the nRF Connect SDK.

if USSLP_ZIGBEE

config USSLP_ZIGBEE_ENDPOINT
	int "USSLP application endpoint"
	default 10

config USSLP_ZIGBEE_ROUTER
	bool "Build as a mains-powered relay rather than a sleepy end device"
	default n
	help
	  A relay keeps its receiver on and routes for its children. It is the same
	  firmware with a different power policy and a larger neighbour table, and
	  it is what makes a 25-metre aisle reachable from one controller.

endif # USSLP_ZIGBEE

config USSLP_BLE
	bool "BLE 5.0 secondary radio"
	default y
	help
	  Commissioning, field diagnostics and the shopper-facing beacon. The BLE
	  controller and the 802.15.4 radio share one antenna, so this costs
	  arbitration time on the primary radio; a store that commissions with NFC
	  only can turn it off and get the airtime back.

config USSLP_REQUIRE_ATTESTATION
	bool "Refuse any price update that does not carry an end-to-end attestation"
	default y
	help
	  INTERFACE-CONTRACTS section 5 places attestation verification at the Shelf
	  Edge Controller, and the Tier 2 to Tier 1 air protocol
	  (edge/labelsim/wire.go) accordingly carries no signature: what crosses the
	  mesh is a sequence number, a price and compressed pixels.

	  That design is sound against the threat model it states — an attacker with
	  write access to the store's broker cannot author a price — but it trusts
	  the controller, and a controller that has been replaced or rooted is inside
	  that trust boundary. With this option the label verifies for itself, using
	  the extended frame type 4 in radio/usslp_wire.h and a key ring it syncs
	  directly.

	  Set it to n for interoperability with controllers that still speak only
	  frame types 1-3. Doing so is a deliberate decision to trust Tier 2, and it
	  is logged at boot so that a fleet audit can find every label running that
	  way.

config USSLP_ATTESTATION_STRICT_CLOCK
	bool "Refuse an attestation when the label has no trusted time"
	default n
	depends on USSLP_REQUIRE_ATTESTATION
	help
	  A label with no clock cannot check a key's validity window. The default is
	  to verify the signature and skip the window, because the signature is the
	  real control and a label that refuses every price until it has synced time
	  is a label that shows nothing after a battery change. Enable this where a
	  trusted time source is guaranteed and a retired key must never be honoured.

config USSLP_KEYRING_SYNC_INTERVAL_HOURS
	int "How often to re-sync the price authority key ring"
	default 24
	range 1 168
	help
	  pki.PriceAuthority rotates with an overlap window. A label that syncs less
	  often than the overlap will meet a key it does not have and refuse prices
	  until it catches up.

config USSLP_BEACON_FAST_MS
	int "Listen interval while active"
	default 250
	help
	  The interval that makes a price change reach the glass inside the
	  platform's 300 ms budget. It is affordable only inside an activity window;
	  held all day it costs 208 uA and the cell lasts 99 days. See
	  power/usslp_budget.h for the arithmetic.

config USSLP_BEACON_SLOW_MS
	int "Listen interval at rest"
	default 30000
	help
	  The resting interval. A label here is on average half an interval from
	  being reachable at all, which is why a price load is planned as a window
	  rather than fired one label at a time.

config USSLP_ACTIVE_WINDOW_MS
	int "How long to stay on the fast interval after activity"
	default 60000

config USSLP_TARGET_LIFE_MILLIYEARS
	int "Battery life the adaptive policy aims at, in thousandths of a year"
	default 8000
	help
	  The power state machine derives its resting interval from this and the
	  measured workload, rather than from a hard-coded number. A label in a
	  freezer case, or one being repriced hourly, will choose a slower resting
	  interval than one on an ambient shelf.

config USSLP_TELEMETRY_INTERVAL_S
	int "Uplink health report interval"
	default 300
	help
	  Five minutes. Polling 40,000 labels would cost a zone's whole channel;
	  letting each label speak once every five minutes costs under one per cent
	  of it.

config USSLP_NFC
	bool "ST25DV dynamic NFC tag"
	default y

config USSLP_TAMPER
	bool "LIS2DH12 tamper detection"
	default y

config USSLP_OTA
	bool "Over-the-air update over the mesh"
	default y

if USSLP_OTA

config USSLP_OTA_DELTA
	bool "Accept binary delta patches as well as whole images"
	default y

config USSLP_OTA_CONFIRM_TIMEOUT_S
	int "Seconds a new image has to confirm itself before the watchdog reverts it"
	default 600
	help
	  A new image marks itself confirmed only after it has joined the mesh and
	  applied one update. Until then the watchdog is armed and a reset reverts to
	  the previous slot. Ten minutes is long enough to cover a slow mesh rejoin
	  and short enough that a label bricked by a bad image is back on the air
	  within a shift.

endif # USSLP_OTA

module = USSLP
module-str = usslp
source "subsys/logging/Kconfig.template.log_config"

endmenu

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
